<p>The Ethics of Ambiguity, Part Two</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/gettyimages-1912911752-612x612.jpg" alt="Photograph of a crowd of people in an urban setting, with individuals moving in different directions." width="860" height="320" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Part Two is where the argument becomes both a typology of failure and, in its final movement, a positive account of what genuine ethical freedom looks like.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    The typology of inauthentic attitudes
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Part Two opens with a catalogue of ways people flee from genuine freedom. De Beauvoir's method here is phenomenological: she describes recognizable human types, each representing a characteristic structure of evasion. These are not personality types in a pop-psychology sense; they are philosophical descriptions of different ways of misrelating to one's freedom. Any given person might exhibit several of them at different times.</p>
<p><strong>The sub-man</strong> is the attitude of pure passivity. The sub-man does not choose; he drifts, avoids engagement, flees from the weight of decision by letting circumstances carry him along. He does not affirm anything or commit to anything, and he thinks this is neutral. De Beauvoir argues it is not neutral: the refusal to engage is itself a choice, and a particularly dangerous one, because the passive person becomes available to be recruited by whoever is willing to give him a cause. The sub-man is not independent; he is malleable.</p>
<p><strong>The serious man</strong> is in some ways the opposite: highly engaged, committed, productive. But his engagement is directed at an absolute, an objective value or institution to which he subordinates his freedom entirely. He defines himself by the Cause, the Party, the Company, the Nation. The seriousness of his commitment looks like virtue, but de Beauvoir argues it is a form of bad faith: the serious man has offloaded his freedom onto an external object and acts as though the object, not he, is responsible for his choices. When the Cause demands something wrong, the serious man does it, because the Cause is beyond question. This is the psychology that makes institutional atrocity possible.</p>
<p><strong>The nihilist</strong> is what the serious man becomes when the idol fails. Having organized his existence around an absolute, and having discovered that the absolute is empty, he concludes that nothing matters. Nihilism looks like radical freedom, but de Beauvoir argues it is still bad faith: the nihilist is reacting against a lost certainty rather than genuinely assuming freedom. His cool dismissal of everything is not honest acceptance of the human condition; it is wounded seriousness performing indifference.</p>
<p><strong>The adventurer</strong> pursues excitement and novelty for their own sake, embracing the exercise of freedom without caring about its content. He is not passive like the sub-man, not committed to an absolute like the serious man, not paralyzed like the nihilist. But his freedom is empty because it is self-enclosed: it does not aim at anything outside the sensation of freedom itself. The adventurer's existence is ultimately shallow because the freedom he exercises never serves anything beyond itself.</p>
<p><strong>The passionate man</strong> commits himself totally to a person or a project, absorbing his freedom into that object. He differs from the serious man in that his absolute is particular rather than abstract, but the structure is similar: he reduces himself to an instrument of his passion, and this reduction is ultimately a flight from the responsibility of being free.</p>
<p>Reading these five types, it helps to ask: what do they have in common? Each one avoids the full acknowledgment of what freedom actually is. Either they deny that they are free (sub-man, serious man), or they exercise freedom without genuine engagement (adventurer), or they reject freedom itself (nihilist), or they dissolve freedom into a single consuming object (passionate man). None of them lives with freedom honestly, and that dishonesty is, for de Beauvoir, the source of their moral failure.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    To will oneself free is to will others free
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Having described what inauthentic freedom looks like, de Beauvoir turns to the question of what authentic freedom demands. Her answer is the book's central philosophical claim, and it is worth slowing down to understand exactly what she is arguing.</p>
<p>The claim is this: if I genuinely will my own freedom, rather than denying it or fleeing it, I am also committed to willing the freedom of others. This is not a moral commandment added from outside. It follows from what freedom actually is.</p>
<p>Here is how the argument runs. My freedom is not an isolated, self-contained thing. I am a situated being, and my freedom realizes itself through projects undertaken in a shared world. The meaning of my projects depends on the world in which they are carried out, and that world is a world of other free beings. A novelist's work is meaningless if there are no readers capable of engaging with it freely. A political activist's struggle is meaningless if the people she is fighting for cannot themselves exercise genuine freedom once conditions change. A teacher's achievement is hollow if the students are merely absorbing information rather than thinking for themselves. In each case, my freedom, when genuinely exercised toward its own ends, requires others to be free.</p>
<p>But the argument goes further. If I will my freedom and my freedom requires others to be free, then I cannot be genuinely indifferent to others' freedom without being incoherent. The person who says "I care about my freedom but not about yours" has not understood what they are willing when they will freedom. Freedom, for de Beauvoir, is not a private possession. It is a condition that only fully exists in a world where it is recognized and supported.</p>
<p>This is a demanding and contestable claim, and you should think carefully about whether it holds. Does de Beauvoir's argument actually establish what she wants it to, or is there a gap between "my freedom depends on others being free in some respects" and "I am obligated to actively will others' freedom in all respects"? The reflection prompt will ask you to work this out.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Oppression as a moral failure
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>One of the important consequences of de Beauvoir's argument is a philosophical account of why oppression is wrong. On her view, oppression is not simply unjust in the utilitarian sense of producing more suffering than freedom would, nor simply in violation of a Kantian principle of treating persons as ends. It is wrong because it is a denial of freedom, and denying freedom contradicts the structure of what freedom is.</p>
<p>The oppressor who treats other people as instruments, as objects to be used for his purposes, is not simply being unkind. He is performing a kind of philosophical violence: treating as things what are in fact free consciousnesses. And in doing so, he also diminishes his own freedom, because the meaning of his projects depends on being recognized by free beings, not validated by subjugated ones. A tyrant who rules through terror does not actually achieve the recognition he seeks, because the recognition of those who are afraid is not genuine recognition. The oppressor's bad faith goes all the way down.</p>
<p>This argument is one of the most original things in the book. It is not merely an argument against oppression from the outside, from some external moral standard. It is an argument that oppression is self-defeating on the oppressor's own terms, given what freedom is. You cannot genuinely secure your own freedom by eliminating others', because freedom is not that kind of thing.</p>
<p>The same analysis applies to the person who accepts oppression passively. The oppressed who internalizes their oppression, who says "this is just the way things are" and makes no attempt to exercise whatever freedom they retain, is also in bad faith. De Beauvoir is careful to note that this is not a moral condemnation of people in genuinely difficult circumstances; the weight of structural oppression is real and she acknowledges it. But she insists that there is a meaningful distinction between accepting one's situation honestly, as the starting point from which to project beyond it, and internalizing it as if it were one's nature.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    Where the course ends up
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>De Beauvoir brings the course to a close at a specific place. The question this course opened with, where does morality come from and does it have an objective foundation, has received six very different answers.</p>
<p>Aristotle said morality is grounded in human nature and the teleological structure of a good life. Aquinas said it is grounded in eternal law, accessible through natural reason and divine revelation. Kant said it is grounded in pure reason, in the structure of rationality itself as universally applicable to all rational agents. Mill said it is grounded in the observable facts of what makes creatures like us flourish and suffer. Nietzsche said none of these foundations holds up under honest historical scrutiny, and that the values we inherited are products of resentment rather than insight.</p>
<p>De Beauvoir accepts Nietzsche's debunking but refuses his conclusion. She agrees that there is no fixed essence, no external authority, no rational structure given in advance that settles moral questions before we ask them. But she argues that this does not leave us without a foothold. Freedom, honestly assumed rather than fled, has a structure. That structure is not compatible with the denial of others' freedom. An ethics built on that foundation is thin compared to Aristotle's rich account of virtue or Aquinas's cosmic order, but it is not nothing. It is the minimum ethics available to beings who have fully accepted that there is no more than that.</p>
<p>Whether the minimum is enough is not a question this course can settle for you. What the course can do, and what it has tried to do, is put you in a position to see the question clearly, to understand what is at stake in each of the available answers, and to think about which of them, or which combination of them, is most honest about the human situation as you understand it. That is the work of moral philosophy, and it is yours to continue.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>← LOOKING BACK AT THE COURSE</p>
<p>Six thinkers, six answers to the same question. Aristotle, Aquinas, Kant, Mill, Nietzsche, de Beauvoir: each one pressed the question of moral foundations further than the last, and each one found something different when they got there. The Final Paper is your chance to show that you understand what one of them found, and why it matters.</p>
